\title{\code{\verb~|-X policy~} selects practical or strict; new load defaults}
\taxon{Change}
\date{2026-11-24}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{changelog}
\tag{launcher-platform}
\tag{unreleased}
\tag{loader}
\meta{release}{Unreleased}
\meta{commit}{c4f8f5d}
\import{base-macros}

\p{One launcher option selects what a run promises, following [the standing policies](standing-policies). \code{\verb~|policy=practical~}, the default, runs the default engine by priority and none of the exactness-only options. \code{\verb~|policy=strict~} runs the reference engine with every exactness-only option the jars of the run have, such as strict metadata in the loader, and the statistics line names which it had. Neither policy can turn a rejection into an acceptance of something underivable.}

\p{The same publication changes the load defaults: pipelined loading for two or more workers on inputs of 8 MiB and more, two scanner threads on mapped input from 256 MiB, and \code{\verb~|--threads auto~} meaning one worker per CPU up to 8. It was published after the gate on exactly these jars in the default configuration, with no option at all: 230 of 230 tests correct, 17,775 mutants with 0 soundness and 0 completeness differences (16,092 agree, 1,683 declined), and seven corpora accepted by the native image.}

\p{Detail: \src{rlvm-main/README.md}, \src{docs/design/OWNER-NOTES.md}. Commit \commit{c4f8f5d}. Session: [exactness](ses-874D). Related: [practical exactness](dec-TQSI), [the exactness contract](design-exactness-contract), [pipelined loading as the default for parallel runs](dec-4TQJ).}
